/* ============================================================================
   DIBSEN Water — ripple and reef detail, taken from real footage.

   Both textures are derived from AI-generated Raja Ampat plates shot to spec
   (see _pipeline/VIDEO-PROMPTS.md, elements E1 and E2), then processed here:

     1. cropped to a region of UNIFORM scale. The source plates are shot at an
        angle, so they bake in their own perspective — fine ripples far, coarse
        ripples near. Tiled onto the 3D sea that baked perspective fights the
        real one, so only a near-field band of even scale is kept.
     2. high-passed against a heavy blur, which strips the low-frequency
        brightness. A tile with a bright or dark patch in it announces itself
        the moment it repeats; a tile carrying only structure does not.
     3. made seamless by Moisan periodic+smooth decomposition — the periodic
        component tiles by construction, with none of the mirror symmetry a
        flip-tile would introduce.

   WHY BASE64 AND NOT A VIDEO
   The site is tested over file://, where a <video> used as a WebGL texture
   taints the canvas and throws. Same reason the island geometry is embedded in
   islands.js. So there is no video element here at all: the ripple structure is
   one still, and the MOTION comes from the shader sampling it twice at
   different scales, directions and speeds. Two scrolling layers never repeat
   together, so the water moves forever from a single 23 KB image.

   ripple  512x512 grey, ~23 KB   reef  512x512 grey, ~17 KB
   ========================================================================== */
